  "worked_solution": "A numerical method \n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+\u0394{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $\u0394x$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda \u0394x){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda \u0394x)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda \u0394x)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda \u0394x>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the implicit Euler method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=-y,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{\u2032}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ the implicit Euler method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1})\\cdot \u0394x.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the implicit Euler method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-{y}_{n+1})\\cdot \u0394x \\\\ {y}_{n+1}+{y}_{n+1}\\cdot \u0394x & ={y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1}(1+\u0394x) & ={y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & =\\frac{1}{1+\u0394x}\\cdot {y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is \n\n$$\nR(\u0394x)=\\frac{1}{1+\u0394x}.\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is true.\n\n- The method is stable when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\u0394x)| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{1}{1+\u0394x}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if\n$|\\frac{1}{1+\u0394x}|<1.$\nHence, statement II is true.\n\n- Now, since $1+\u0394x>1$ for any positive step size $\u0394x,$ we have\n\n$$\n|\\frac{1}{1+\u0394x}|<1\n$$\n\nfor all $\u0394x>0.$ So, the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, and statement III is true.\n\n- Finally, because the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, it is stable for step size $\u0394x=0.50.$ Hence, statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only\".",
